  "body": {
    "id": "prop:not-uniform-rerandomization-design-class",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every finite \\(a>0\\), over the outcome-free covariate-design class \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{PSRR}}^{x}\\), the original first-entry PSRR covariate limit \\(C_{k,a}\\) is not equal in distribution to the uniform-rerandomization limit \\(L_{k,a}\\).  Specifically,\n\\[\n \\Pr\\{\\|C_{k,a}\\|^2=a\\}=1-F_{\\chi_k^2}(a)>0,\n \\qquad\n \\Pr\\{\\|L_{k,a}\\|^2=a\\}=0.\n\\]\nThe class \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{PSRR}}^{x}\\) is a regular equal-allocation subclass of the arrays admitted by the original Zhu--Liu first-entry algorithm.  Therefore this subclass certificate also refutes, on the broader published class, any universal representation of the original algorithm's output as a uniform draw from the acceptable set.  The positive diffusion assertion itself remains restricted to \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{PSRR}}^{x}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "covariateDim",
      "covariateDesignClass",
      "threshold",
      "clippedGaussian",
      "truncatedGaussian"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:psrr-class",
      "def:original-psrr",
      "thm:radial-entrance-law-design-class",
      "def:clipped-gaussian"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The clipped-Gaussian definition and continuity of the chi-square distribution give\n\\[\n \\Pr\\{\\|C_{k,a}\\|^2=a\\}\n =\\Pr\\{\\|G\\|^2\\ge a\\}\n =1-F_{\\chi_k^2}(a)>0. \\tag{61}\n\\]\nBy contrast,\n\\[\n L_{k,a}\\overset d=G\\mid\\{\\|G\\|^2\\le a\\}.\n\\]\nIts conditional squared radius is the continuous chi-square law restricted to \\([0,a]\\), so\n\\[\n \\Pr\\{\\|L_{k,a}\\|^2=a\\}=0. \\tag{62}\n\\]\nThe measurable boundary sphere \\(\\{d:\\|d\\|^2=a\\}\\) separates the two probability measures, proving that the laws differ.\n\nEvery design in \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{PSRR}}^x\\) has equal arms, uniform balanced initialization, treated--control transposition proposals, the original Metropolis acceptance rule with fixed finite tuning, and immediate stopping at the first entry into \\(M_n\\le a\\).  These are precisely the operations in the original PSRR definition.  Boundedness, fixed dimension, second-moment convergence, and covariance nondegeneracy only restrict the deterministic covariate arrays on which that algorithm is run.  Hence \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{PSRR}}^x\\) is a subclass of the broader published algorithmic domain.  Were the original algorithm's terminal output uniformly distributed on the acceptable set throughout that broader domain, its asymptotic covariate law would be \\(L_{k,a}\\) on every sequence in this subclass, contradicting (61)--(62).  This subclass witness refutes the universal uniform representation; it does not enlarge the positive functional-limit result beyond \\(\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{PSRR}}^x\\).",
    "justification": "A positive boundary atom is a computable measure-separating certificate and does not rely on outcome assumptions.",
    "gap": "Stationary or correction-based uniformity results concern changed output mechanisms and cannot represent the original first-entry terminal allocation.",
    "consumer": "Analysts can rule out truncated-Gaussian calibration for original first-entry PSRR; by subclass inclusion, the certificate refutes a universal uniform representation over the broader published algorithmic class.",
